WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 put Orontes's breakeven at 2,630,000 jars, or $31,560,000 of sales, against actual sales of 3,500,000 jars and $42,000,000. Chapter 3 showed that the breakeven moves with the mix, and under the company's real three-product mix it is 33,221,100. Book 1 measured a degree of operating leverage of 4.02. This chapter is what those figures are for.
4.1  The margin of safety
The margin of safety is the amount by which sales exceed the breakeven. Below that amount the company still makes money; past it, it does not.
For Orontes on the single-product figures: 42,000,000 − 31,560,000 = 10,440,000 of sales, which as a proportion is 10,440,000 ÷ 42,000,000 = 24.86 per cent. In units it is 3,500,000 − 2,630,000 = 870,000 jars.
So Orontes can lose a quarter of its sales before it loses money. That is a usable sentence for a board, and it is the single most useful figure in Section C, because it converts a cost structure into a statement about risk.
On the three-product mix of Chapter 3 the picture is tighter: the breakeven is 33,221,100 of sales, so the margin of safety is 42,000,000 − 33,221,100 = 8,778,900, which is 8,778,900 ÷ 42,000,000 = 20.90 per cent. Four points of safety disappear once the lower-margin drum is in the mix, which is the mix effect of Chapter 3 expressed as risk rather than as a breakeven.
Three readings of the ratio, all examinable.
A high margin of safety means a company can absorb a bad year. A low one means a small fall in demand turns a profit into a loss.
The margin of safety and the degree of operating leverage are the same fact from two directions. Orontes's leverage of 4.02 says a 10 per cent sales fall costs 40 per cent of the profit; its margin of safety of 24.86 per cent says the profit disappears entirely at a fall of about a quarter. Both come from the same two numbers, the contribution margin and the fixed costs, and the exam may ask for either.
A margin of safety is meaningful only against the mix and the relevant range assumed. A company that would shed fixed costs if volume fell by a quarter has a larger true margin than the arithmetic shows.
Figure F704-02 compares the two companies of these books.
Figure F704-02. Margin of safety, two companies
@table
Measure | Orontes, single product | Orontes, three-product mix | Barada
Sales | 42,000,000 | 42,000,000 | 21,978,000
Breakeven sales | 31,560,000 | 33,221,100 | 17,328,000
Margin of safety | 10,440,000 | 8,778,900 | 4,650,000
Margin of safety ratio | 24.86% | 20.90% | 21.16%
Degree of operating leverage | 4.02 | 4.02 | 4.73
@end
Barada's position repays a second look. Its margin of safety ratio of 21.16 per cent looks much like Orontes's, and its operating leverage of 4.73 is worse — and the figure that matters most is in neither column: its contribution margin ratio is 12 per cent against Orontes's 40. Barada has to replace eight dollars of lost sales to recover one dollar of lost contribution, where Orontes replaces two and a half. A margin of safety says how far away the cliff is; it does not say how hard the climb back up would be.
