4.2  Sensitivity analysis
Every figure in Chapters 1 to 3 was treated as known. None of them is. The price is a forecast, the variable cost depends on the sesame market, the fixed costs depend on decisions not yet taken, and the volume is the least certain of the four. Sensitivity analysis asks how much the conclusion depends on each.
Three techniques, in rising order of effort, and the exam names all three.
One-at-a-time sensitivity. Change a single input by a stated amount and see what happens. Chapter 2's Figure F702-05 was exactly this: five per cent on each of four inputs, which showed the price to be the most powerful and the fixed cost the least. The output is a ranking of what matters.
Scenario analysis. Change several inputs together in a way that makes a coherent story — a bad year has lower volume and lower prices and probably higher input costs, because they tend to arrive together. One-at-a-time analysis cannot capture that, and a scenario can.
Simulation. Assign a distribution to each input and let a computer draw thousands of combinations, producing a distribution of outcomes rather than three. Chapter 15 returns to it under the name Monte Carlo simulation; it is not examined computationally here.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: three scenarios for Orontes
Step 1. The base case. 3,500,000 jars at a $4.80 contribution gives 3,500,000 × 4.80 = 16,800,000 of contribution, and 16,800,000 − 12,624,000 = 4,176,000 of operating income.
Step 2. The pessimistic case. Volume falls 10 per cent to 3,150,000 jars and the price is cut 2 per cent to $11.76, so the margin becomes 11.76 − 7.20 = 4.56. Contribution is 3,150,000 × 4.56 = 14,364,000 and operating income is 14,364,000 − 12,624,000 = 1,740,000.
Step 3. The optimistic case. Volume rises 8 per cent to 3,780,000 jars at a price 2 per cent higher, $12.24, so the margin is 12.24 − 7.20 = 5.04. Contribution is 3,780,000 × 5.04 = 19,051,200 and operating income is 19,051,200 − 12,624,000 = 6,427,200.
Step 4. The expected value. At probabilities of 0.25, 0.50 and 0.25: 0.25 × 1,740,000 = 435,000, 0.50 × 4,176,000 = 2,088,000 and 0.25 × 6,427,200 = 1,606,800, which add to 435,000 + 2,088,000 + 1,606,800 = 4,129,800.
Check. The expected value is close to the base case and the spread around it is enormous: from $1,740,000 to $6,427,200, a range nearly as large as the expected value itself. That spread is the finding, not the expectation, and an answer that quotes only the $4,129,800 has hidden the thing the board needed to know — which is Chapter 1 of Book 2 restated in a Section C setting.
Figure F704-03 sets the scenarios out.
Figure F704-03. Three scenarios, one cost structure
@table
Scenario | Volume | Price | Contribution margin | Operating income | Probability
Pessimistic | 3,150,000 | 11.76 | 4.56 | 1,740,000 | 0.25
Base | 3,500,000 | 12.00 | 4.80 | 4,176,000 | 0.50
Optimistic | 3,780,000 | 12.24 | 5.04 | 6,427,200 | 0.25
Expected value | | | | 4,129,800 | 1.00
@end
EXAM TRAP   Scenarios and single variables
A scenario changes several inputs together. Changing one at a time and adding the results double-counts and usually overstates the range.
An expected value is not an outcome. It is a weighted average of outcomes, and it may itself be impossible.
Where a question gives probabilities, use them; where it does not, present the range and say which end you regard as more likely and why.
