4.4  Making the recommendation
The outcome asks for a course of action, and an answer that stops at a number does not give one. Five elements turn an analysis into a recommendation, and they are worth learning as a checklist because the exam rewards each of them.
State the decision. "Automate" or "do not automate", in the first sentence, not the last. A marker should not have to search for the answer.
Support it with the number that drives it. Not every number computed — the one that decides. For the automation question that is the indifference volume of 3,000,000 jars against actual volume of 3,500,000, not the $250,000 of annual gain.
Name what the decision depends on. Every recommendation in this chapter rests on an assumption: the volume holds, the mix holds, the price holds, the fixed cost estimate is right. Say which one would reverse the answer if it failed, and at what value. "Automation is right above 3,000,000 jars and wrong below it" is worth more than three paragraphs of hedging.
Give the qualitative factors their place. The numbers do not capture the effect of automation on a workforce, on flexibility, on the ability to make small batches, or on a supplier relationship. Chapter 8 makes this point formally for make-or-buy decisions; it applies everywhere.
Say what you would do next. Test the volume assumption with the sales force, obtain a second quotation for the equipment, run the decision again at the pessimistic scenario. A recommendation with a next step is a recommendation somebody can act on.
Figure F704-05 applies the checklist to Orontes.
Figure F704-05. A recommendation, in five parts
@table
Element | For the automation decision
The decision | Automate, subject to the volume test below
The deciding number | Indifference volume of 3,000,000 jars against 3,500,000 actual
What it depends on | Volume staying above 3,000,000; the equipment costing what was quoted
What would reverse it | A fall of more than 14 per cent in volume, or fixed costs above 15,300,000
The qualitative factors | Loss of flexibility in small batches; effect on the packing workforce
The next step | Re-run at the pessimistic scenario of 3,150,000 jars before committing
@end
One last habit closes the chapter, and it is the one that most distinguishes a professional answer. Say what the analysis does not show. Cost-volume-profit analysis is silent about the competitor's response, about what the customers will think of a price change, about whether the sales force can deliver the volume, and about everything beyond the relevant range. A candidate who names the silence is telling a marker that the model is understood, not merely applied.
