Chapter summary
Figure F704-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F704-07. Chapter 4 summary map: the key rule for each section.
The margin of safety is how far sales stand above the breakeven: 42,000,000 − 31,560,000 = 10,440,000 for Orontes, which is 10,440,000 ÷ 42,000,000 = 24.86 per cent, or 870,000 jars — and under the real three-product mix it falls to 20.90 per cent, which is the mix effect expressed as risk. It is the same fact as the operating leverage of 4.02, seen from the other side. Sensitivity analysis asks how much the answer depends on each input: one at a time, which ranks them; by scenario, which lets several move together, giving Orontes a range from $1,740,000 to $6,427,200 around an expected value of 4,129,800; and by simulation, which produces a distribution. Two cost structures are compared at their indifference volume, which for the automation choice is 1,500,000 ÷ 0.50 = 3,000,000 jars against an actual 3,500,000 — so automation wins today, by 500,000 × 0.50 = 250,000 a year, and raises the breakeven permanently. And a recommendation states the decision first, supports it with the one number that decides, names the value at which it would reverse, gives the qualitative factors their place, proposes a next step, and says what the model cannot see.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P4-01  The margin of safety equals:
A.	sales less breakeven sales.
B.	sales less variable costs.
C.	contribution margin less fixed costs.
D.	breakeven sales.
P4-02  Sales of 12,000,000 with a breakeven of 8,000,000 give a margin of safety ratio of:
A.	33.3 per cent
B.	66.7 per cent
C.	4,000,000 per cent
D.	50.0 per cent
P4-03  A company with a low margin of safety:
A.	loses money after a small fall in demand.
B.	has low fixed costs.
C.	has a high contribution margin.
D.	cannot break even.
P4-04  Scenario analysis:
A.	changes several inputs together coherently.
B.	changes one input at a time.
C.	removes uncertainty.
D.	requires probabilities.
P4-05  An expected value is:
A.	a probability-weighted average that may itself be impossible.
B.	the most likely single outcome.
C.	the best case.
D.	the worst case.
P4-06  The indifference volume between two structures is where:
A.	both give the same operating income.
B.	both break even.
C.	fixed costs match.
D.	contribution margins match.
P4-07  Extra fixed costs of 900,000 and extra contribution of 1.50 a unit give an indifference volume of:
A.	600,000 units
B.	1,350,000 units
C.	900,000 units
D.	60,000 units
P4-08  Above the indifference volume, the structure with higher fixed costs and a higher margin:
A.	earns more.
B.	earns less.
C.	breaks even sooner.
D.	is identical.
P4-09  A recommendation is strongest when it names:
A.	the value of the key assumption at which the answer would reverse.
B.	every number computed.
C.	the auditors' view.
D.	the tax rate.
P4-10  Cost-volume-profit analysis cannot tell you:
A.	how competitors will react to a price change.
B.	the breakeven point.
C.	the contribution margin.
D.	operating income at a volume.
Case-style practice: a price cut, tested
Orontes's sales director returns with the price-cut proposal of Chapter 2 in a new form: cut the price by 40 cents and expect 8 per cent more volume, with a one-in-three chance that volume rises only 3 per cent and a one-in-six chance that it rises 14 per cent. The jar sells at $12.00 with variable costs of $7.20, fixed costs are $12,624,000, and current volume is 3,500,000 jars.
Required. Compute the operating income under each outcome and the expected value. Say what volume the price cut needs to break even against the present position, and make a recommendation. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC4-1  Answer A. Sales above the breakeven.
B is wrong. That is contribution margin.
C is wrong. That subtracts two unrelated figures.
D is wrong. That is operating income.
SC4-2  Answer A. 10,440,000 ÷ 42,000,000 = 24.86 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the breakeven as a share of sales.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. The margin of safety in dollars is not a percentage.
SC4-3  Answer A. The profit disappears quickly.
B is wrong. Low fixed costs usually produce a high margin of safety.
C is wrong. A high margin would widen the safety, not narrow it.
D is wrong. A company above breakeven is profitable at any margin of safety.
SC4-4  Answer A. Coherent combinations of several inputs.
B is wrong. That is one-at-a-time sensitivity.
C is wrong. Every scenario is built on assumptions.
D is wrong. Scenarios produce several answers.
SC4-5  Answer A. A weighted average need not be a possible outcome.
B is wrong. The most likely outcome here is the base case.
C is wrong. The best case is 6,427,200.
D is wrong. The breakeven is a volume, not an income.
SC4-6  Answer A. Knowing which input matters is the point.
B is wrong. The expected value hides the spread.
C is wrong. The count of scenarios is not a finding.
D is wrong. Tax is not part of this analysis.
SC4-7  Answer A. The same operating income under both.
B is wrong. The two structures break even at different volumes.
C is wrong. The fixed costs differ by construction.
D is wrong. The margins differ by construction.
SC4-8  Answer A. 1,500,000 ÷ 0.50 = 3,000,000 units.
B is wrong. This divides by 2.00.
C is wrong. That is the extra fixed cost in dollars.
D is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
SC4-9  Answer A. High fixed costs reward volume and punish volatility.
B is wrong. Volatile demand suits a lower breakeven.
C is wrong. Low volumes favour the variable structure.
D is wrong. A falling market is the worst case for high fixed costs.
SC4-10  Answer A. State the decision first.
B is wrong. Calculations support the decision; they are not it.
C is wrong. Assumptions qualify the decision.
D is wrong. Qualitative factors come after the decision is stated.
SC4-11  Answer A. A reversal value is specific and testable.
B is wrong. A list is not a conclusion.
C is wrong. That sentence tells a reader nothing.
D is wrong. The outcome asks for a recommendation.
SC4-12  Answer A. The model has no competitor or customer in it.
B is wrong. The margin is an input to the model.
C is wrong. Fixed costs are an input.
D is wrong. The breakeven is its principal output.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Breakeven in sales | 8,000,000: 2,400,000 ÷ 0.30 = 8,000,000.
Margin of safety | 4,000,000: 12,000,000 − 8,000,000 = 4,000,000.
Margin of safety ratio | 33.3 per cent: 4,000,000 ÷ 12,000,000 = 33.3 per cent.
Operating income | 1,200,000: contribution is 12,000,000 × 0.30 = 3,600,000, less fixed costs of 2,400,000.
Indifference volume | 600,000 units: 900,000 ÷ 1.50 = 600,000.
Which structure at 700,000 units | The higher-fixed structure, because 700,000 is above the indifference volume of 600,000 — it earns 100,000 × 1.50 = 150,000 more a year.
What it tells you | The company has a third of its sales as safety and sits 100,000 units above the point at which the heavier structure pays. Both margins are comfortable, and the recommendation would change if volume fell below 600,000 units, which is a 14 per cent fall.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P4-01  Answer A. Sales above the breakeven.
B is wrong. That is contribution margin.
C is wrong. That is operating income.
D is wrong. The breakeven is the reference point, not the margin.
P4-02  Answer A. 4,000,000 ÷ 12,000,000 = 33.3 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the breakeven's share of sales.
C is wrong. The dollar margin is not a percentage.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
P4-03  Answer A. A small fall removes the profit.
B is wrong. Low fixed costs widen the margin of safety.
C is wrong. A high margin widens it too.
D is wrong. The company is above breakeven, by definition of having a margin.
P4-04  Answer A. Several inputs move together.
B is wrong. That is one-at-a-time analysis.
C is wrong. Uncertainty is described, not removed.
D is wrong. Probabilities are useful and not essential.
P4-05  Answer A. A weighted average may be impossible.
B is wrong. The most likely outcome is the one with the highest probability.
C is wrong. The best case is one of the inputs.
D is wrong. So is the worst.
P4-06  Answer A. Equal operating income.
B is wrong. Their breakevens differ.
C is wrong. The fixed costs differ by construction.
D is wrong. The margins differ by construction.
P4-07  Answer A. 900,000 ÷ 1.50 = 600,000 units.
B is wrong. This multiplies instead of dividing.
C is wrong. That is the extra fixed cost.
D is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
P4-08  Answer A. Above the indifference volume the heavier structure wins.
B is wrong. That is the position below it.
C is wrong. Its breakeven is higher.
D is wrong. They are identical only at the indifference volume.
P4-09  Answer A. A reversal value is actionable.
B is wrong. Listing every number buries the decision.
C is wrong. Auditors do not decide operating questions.
D is wrong. Tax does not change the ranking here.
P4-10  Answer A. The model contains no competitor.
B is wrong. The breakeven is its main output.
C is wrong. The margin is an input.
D is wrong. Income at a volume is a direct output.
Case-style set: a price cut, tested
The three outcomes. The price falls to $11.60, so the contribution margin becomes 11.60 − 7.20 = 4.40.
At 3 per cent more volume, 3,605,000 jars: contribution is 3,605,000 × 4.40 = 15,862,000, and operating income is 15,862,000 − 12,624,000 = 3,238,000.
At 8 per cent, 3,780,000 jars: 3,780,000 × 4.40 = 16,632,000, giving 16,632,000 − 12,624,000 = 4,008,000.
At 14 per cent, 3,990,000 jars: 3,990,000 × 4.40 = 17,556,000, giving 17,556,000 − 12,624,000 = 4,932,000.
The expected value. At probabilities of one third, one half and one sixth: 0.3333 × 3,238,000 = 1,079,254, 0.5 × 4,008,000 = 2,004,000 and 0.1667 × 4,932,000 = 822,164, which add to about $3,905,000 — below the $4,176,000 the company earns today.
What the cut needs. To hold operating income at $4,176,000 the company needs 12,624,000 + 4,176,000 = 16,800,000 of contribution, which at $4.40 a jar is 16,800,000 ÷ 4.40 = 3,818,182 jars — a volume rise of 9.1 per cent, more than the 8 per cent the sales director expects at his own central case.
Recommendation: reject the price cut. It loses money at the expected volume, loses heavily at the pessimistic one, and gains materially only in the one-in-six case. The deciding number is the 9.1 per cent volume rise it needs to stand still, against an expectation of 8. I would ask the sales director what evidence supports the 8 per cent, since the whole proposal turns on a figure that is below its own breakeven; and I would point out that the cut, once made, is permanent in a way the volume is not.
